# Firebase Configuration Instructions
#
# This file contains instructions for setting up your Firebase project to work with the application.
# 
# 1. Create a Firebase project at https://console.firebase.google.com/
# 2. Click on "Add project" and follow the setup instructions
# 3. After creating your project, click on the web app icon (</>) to create a web app
# 4. Register your app with a nickname (e.g., "browser-assist")
# 5. Copy the Firebase configuration values and paste them in your .env file:

# Firebase Web SDK Configuration Values
FIREBASE_API_KEY="your-api-key" # Get from Firebase console
FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN="your-project-id.firebaseapp.com"
FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID="your-project-id"
FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET="your-project-id.appspot.com"
FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID="your-messaging-sender-id"
FIREBASE_APP_ID="your-app-id"

# Firestore Database Setup
# 
# 1. In your Firebase project, go to "Firestore Database" in the left sidebar
# 2. Click "Create database"
# 3. Choose "Start in test mode" for development (you can secure it later)
# 4. Select a location closest to your users
# 5. Click "Enable"
#
# The application will automatically create the following collections:
# - "messages": Stores all chat messages
# - "sessions": Stores session information
#
# No additional configuration is required for these collections.

# Security Rules (Production)
#
# When moving to production, update your Firestore rules to secure data:
# Go to Firestore Database > Rules and add appropriate security rules.
# Example secure rules (customize based on your authentication method):
#
# ```
# rules_version = '2';
# service cloud.firestore {
#   match /databases/{database}/documents {
#     match /messages/{messageId} {
#       // Only authenticated users can read/write messages
#       allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
#     }
#     match /sessions/{sessionId} {
#       // Only authenticated users can read/write sessions
#       allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
#     }
#   }
# }
# ``` 
